device/usb-video: the screen out of the USB-C port, as a webcam

What it is: the Pro¹x cannot drive a display from its USB-C port (no
DisplayPort in the SM6115, rag-notes display 2026-11-22), and the only USB
display drivers need an adapter.  What a computer, TV or capture program
takes from a USB device without a driver or an install is a webcam: the
USB Video Class.  So the phone becomes a composite USB gadget with its
usual network and serial functions plus a UVC camera whose picture is the
screen, encoded by Venus as H.264 (frame-based UVC format) or by the CPU
as MJPEG.  On the computer, any program that opens a camera shows the
phone: ffplay, mpv, OBS, guvcview, a browser's camera picker, Zoom.

Pieces:
  pro1x-usb-gadget   root; switches the gadget: video (configfs: ECM
                     network, ACM serial, UVC) or network (the built-in
                     g_cdc, the default and rescue link).  Same usb0, same
                     MAC addresses, 172.16.42.1 as before.
  pro1x-uvc-feed     answers the host's UVC requests on the gadget's V4L2
                     device, and while the host streams runs a producer at
                     the committed size, rate and format, sending each
                     frame of its output as a UVC frame.  -t: a dry run
                     that parses a stream from stdin, no device.
  pro1x-usb-video    the user's command: start, stop, status; the producer
                     is pro1x-screencast (H.264, device/stream) or
                     wf-recorder (MJPEG).
  Guix: package pro1x-usb-video (modules/fxtec-pro1x/packages/usb-video.scm),
  service pro1x-usb-video-service-type (services/usb-video.scm): the
  package in the profile and a Shepherd service, `herd start
  pro1x-usb-video` switches the gadget to video, `herd stop` back.

Kernel: CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS_F_UVC=y (device/kernel/pro1x-7.3.fragment);
everything else needed (USB_CONFIGFS=m, F_ECM, F_ACM, LIBCOMPOSITE,
CONFIGFS_FS, VIDEO_DEV) is already in config.aarch64.  Needs a CI kernel
build before the first test.

Why not the alternatives:
  - DisplayPort Alt Mode: no DP controller, USB-only PHY (hardware).
  - GUD or DisplayLink: a USB *display* adapter with a free driver; needs
    hardware.  The GUD *gadget* side (samcday/gud-gadget, a FunctionFS
    implementation in Rust; the kernel f_gud_drm was never merged) would
    make the phone a second screen of the computer, the opposite of what
    is wanted.
  - wl-cast over usb0: already works (`wl-cast start -v usb`), needs a
    browser on the computer and a page to open; the UVC gadget needs
    nothing.  Both can coexist: the network stays in the composite gadget.
  - MHL: no HDMI transmitter in the SoC or the device tree.

Limits known from the source (Linux 7.3 f_uvc):
  - The controller negotiates high speed on the SSH cable (480 Mbit/s,
    isochronous 3 x 1024 bytes per microframe, about 24 MB/s); the UDC is
    super-speed capable (sysfs maximum_speed), so a USB 3 cable and port
    may give more.  H.264 at 2-8 Mbit/s needs none of it; MJPEG 1600x800
    at 30 fps about 10-15 MB/s.
  - Frame-based (H.264) frames have no dwMaxVideoFrameBufferSize in
    configfs: the kernel keeps 460800 bytes per buffer.  pro1x-uvc-feed
    sends a larger access unit over two buffers; the host's decoder must
    join them (ffmpeg's parser does; untested).  Keep the bit rate such
    that keyframes stay under it (4 Mbit/s at 1600x800 should).
  - Isochronous transfers have no retry: a lost packet loses the frame.
    Keyframes every 2 s by default, SPS and PPS repeated before each.
  - Nothing tells the host the screen turns: the frame sizes offered are
    landscape (1600x800, 1280x640, 960x480); pro1x-screencast turns the
    output itself (-t), so a portrait phone shows up turned.

First test, from the author's PC, phone on the cable:
  1. Deploy a generation with the CI kernel that has usb_f_uvc, and the
     pro1x-usb-video service (or the package).  Check on the phone:
       modinfo -n usb_f_uvc
  2. On the phone, from a Wi-Fi SSH session (the USB link drops for a few
     seconds while the gadget re-enumerates):
       sudo pro1x-usb-gadget video     (or: sudo herd start pro1x-usb-video)
       pro1x-usb-gadget status         (expect: configfs, UVC gadget: /dev/videoN)
     The PC should show a new USB device 1d6b:0104 with a video interface
     (lsusb -t) and usb0 back at 172.16.42.1.
  3. On the phone, in the Sway session (or over SSH with
     WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-1 XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000):
       pro1x-usb-video start -v
  4. On the PC:
       v4l2-ctl --list-devices          (find the "Pro1x screen" node)
       v4l2-ctl -d /dev/videoN --list-formats-ext
       ffplay -f v4l2 -input_format h264 -i /dev/videoN
       ffplay -f v4l2 -input_format mjpeg -video_size 1280x640 -i /dev/videoN
       OBS: Video Capture Device (V4L2), the "Pro1x screen" device.
     On the phone, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pro1x-usb-video/feed.log says what the
     host asked for and what was sent.
  5. Back to the plain network gadget:
       pro1x-usb-video stop; sudo pro1x-usb-gadget network

State (2026-11-27): written from the kernel source and the UVC 1.1
specification; the gadget script is syntax-checked, pro1x-uvc-feed
compiles and its frame parsing was tested on the PC with ffmpeg streams
(-t): 15 of 15 PXSC access units, SPS and PPS repeated before each
keyframe, 10 of 10 JPEG pictures, plain Annex B one frame late by
design.  Nothing has run on the phone: its kernel has no usb_f_uvc yet.
